Presidential elections were held in [[Slovakia]] on 15 March 2014, with a second round on 29 March 2014.<ref>{{cite news |editor=Zuzana Vilikovská |title=Paška announces presidential election for March 15 |url=http://spectator.sme.sk/articles/view/52448/10/paska_announces_presidential_election_for_march_15.html |newspaper=[[The Slovak Spectator]] |date=19 December 2013 |access-date=5 January 2014}}</ref>

After a first round in which no candidate secured majority, [[Robert Fico]] and [[Andrej Kiska]] qualified for the second round. On 29 March 2014 Kiska won the second round with 59% of the vote to Fico's 41%.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.sme.sk/prezidentske-volby/2014/vysledky/?ref=ttw|title=Výsledky volieb 2014 - Zistite kto bude novým prezidentom|work=www.sme.sk|access-date=28 March 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150402121537/http://www.sme.sk/prezidentske-volby/2014/vysledky/?ref=ttw|archive-date=2 April 2015|url-status=dead}}</ref>

Kiska succeeded incumbent [[Ivan Gašparovič]], who was unable to run due to [[term limits]] established by the [[Constitution of Slovakia|Constitution]].<ref>{{cite web|title=An unofficial translation of the constitution of the Slovak Republic|url=http://www.slovak-republic.org/constitution/|access-date=31 August 2013|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130901074511/http://www.slovak-republic.org/constitution/|archive-date=1 September 2013}}</ref>

==Candidates==
All candidates who wished to contest the presidency were required to collect no less than 15.000 individual signatures in order to qualify for the race. In addition they had to officially submit their candidacy to the election commission in writing no later than 9 January 2014. At least one would-be candidate, Leonid Chovanec, a citizen from [[Košice]] who intended to run as an independent candidate, gave up his candidacy on January 6 citing "unequal conditions for people in their access to the candidacy for the post of president”. He did not manage to collect the 15,000 signatures to support his presidential bid.<ref>{{cite news|last=Terenzani|first=Michaela|title=Time nearly up for presidential candidates|url=http://spectator.sme.sk/articles/view/52538/10/time_nearly_up_for_presidential_candidates.html|access-date=31 March 2014|newspaper=Slovak Spectator}}</ref> On January 9, Speaker of parliament [[Pavol Paska]] officially announced that the following candidates had been approved.<ref>{{cite news |last=Terenzani|first=Michaela|title=All fifteen candidates  now officially in presidential race|url=http://spectator.sme.sk/articles/view/52622/10/all_15_candidates_now_officially_in_presidential_race.html|access-date=31 March 2014|newspaper=Slovak Spectator}}</ref>

*[[Gyula Bárdos]] – member of the [[Party of the Hungarian Community]]
*{{ill|Jozef Behýl|sk}} – businessman and civil activist
*[[Ján Čarnogurský]] – former chair of the [[Christian Democratic Movement]] 
*[[Robert Fico]] – incumbent prime minister, nominee of [[Direction – Social Democracy]]
*{{ill|Viliam Fischer|sk}} – cardiovascular surgeon
*[[Pavol Hrušovský]] – conservative politician of [[Christian Democratic Movement]]
*{{ill|Ján Jurišta|sk}} – former ambassador to Argentina, supported by the [[Communist Party of Slovakia]]
*[[Andrej Kiska]] – former businessman and philanthropist
*[[Milan Kňažko]] – actor and leader of the [[Velvet Revolution]]
*{{ill|Stanislav Martinčko|sk}} – businessman and chair of the {{ill|Slovakia, do this!|sk|SLOVENSKO DO TOHO !|lt=Coalition of Citizens of Slovakia}}
*{{ill|Milan Melník|sk}} – scientist in [[Coordination Chemistry|coordination]] and [[Bioinorganic Chemistry|bioinorganic chemistry]]
*{{ill|Helena Mezenská|sk}} – MP for the [[Ordinary People and Independent Personalities]]
*[[Radoslav Procházka]] – constitutional lawyer and former member of the Christian Democratic Movement
*{{ill|Jozef Šimko|sk}} – mayor of [[Rimavská Sobota]]

;Withdrawn candidates
*{{ill|Ľubica Blašková|sk}} – entered the presidential race on 7 April 2013, but stepped down on 9 January 2014.
*[[Peter Osuský]] – entered the race on 12 June 2013, but withdrew on 29 January 2014 and his nominational party [[Freedom and Solidarity]] then favourised Radoslav Procházka.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.sme.sk/c/7083621/osusky-sa-vzdal-kandidatury-sas-podporila-prochazku.html|title=SME.sk - Osuský sa vzdal kandidatúry, SaS podporila Procházku|work=www.sme.sk|access-date=28 March 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150402131714/http://www.sme.sk/c/7083621/osusky-sa-vzdal-kandidatury-sas-podporila-prochazku.html|archive-date=2 April 2015|url-status=dead}}</ref>

==Campaign==
The political right was fragmented; the People's Platform (an alliance of three right-wing parties)<ref name="auto">{{cite web|title=Presidential race: no strong candidate yet|url=http://spectator.sme.sk/articles/view/50687|access-date=31 August 2013}}</ref> failed to agree on a common candidate. [[Christian Democratic Movement]] and [[Most-Hid]] announced that they supported Pavol Hrušovský, but the third party, [[Slovak Democratic and Christian Union – Democratic Party]] (SDKÚ-DS) originally refused to agree.<ref name="auto1">{{cite web|title=Opposition Still Opposed on Regional and Presidential Candidates|url=http://www.thedaily.sk/opposition-still-opposed-on-regional-and-presidential-candidates/|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131113045457/http://www.thedaily.sk/opposition-still-opposed-on-regional-and-presidential-candidates/|url-status=dead|archive-date=13 November 2013|access-date=31 August 2013}}</ref> According to multiple sources, SDKÚ would have preferred former Prime Minister [[Iveta Radičová]] as their candidate, however, she made clear that she did not want to run for president again.<ref name="auto1"/><ref>{{cite web|title=Szlovák elnökválasztás: esélytelenek az ismeretlen ellen|url=http://kitekinto.hu/karpat-medence/2013/07/12/szlovak_elnokvalasztas_eselytelenek_az_ismeretlen_ellen/|access-date=31 August 2013}}</ref> Later, after Fico announced its candidacy, SDKÚ-DS eventually decided to support Hrušovský. Shortly before first round of elections some members of SDKÚ-DS and Most-Híd suggested their supporters should choose different candidate in the upcoming elections, following polls which gave Hrušovský only small chance of progressing to second round.

===Robert Fico===
The ruling [[Direction – Social Democracy|Smer]] party selected incumbent Prime Minister [[Robert Fico]] as its official candidate. According to opinion polls, Fico dominated his opponents, who were divided, and he would advance to the second round with a double digit lead.<ref>{{cite news|title=Another poll shows Kiska facing Fico in run-off|url=http://spectator.sme.sk/articles/view/52918/10/another_poll_shows_kiska_facing_fico_in_run_off.html|access-date=31 March 2014|newspaper=Slovak Spectator}}</ref>  In addition, polls showed that he was most likely to win the presidential election.<ref>{{cite web|title=Elnökválasztás: Robert Fico és Andrej Kiska közül választhatnánk a második fordulóban|url=http://www.parameter.sk/rovat/belfold/2013/07/25/elnokvalasztas-robert-fico-es-andrej-kiska-kozul-valaszthatnank-masodik|access-date=31 August 2013|archive-date=21 April 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140421104307/http://www.parameter.sk/rovat/belfold/2013/07/25/elnokvalasztas-robert-fico-es-andrej-kiska-kozul-valaszthatnank-masodik|url-status=dead}}</ref> However, his party would have had to replace him as PM and party leader, meaning that Smer would have had to produce a popular and credible leader to follow Fico.<ref name="auto"/> As early as February, Fico was endorsed by the [[National trade union center]] when the [[Confederation of Trade Unions of the Slovak Republic|Confederation of Labour Unions]] (KOZ) officially called upon its members to vote for Prime Minister Robert Fico.<ref>{{cite news|title=Labour unionists endorse Fico for president|url=http://spectator.sme.sk/articles/view/53017/10/labour_unionists_endorse_fico_for_president.html|access-date=31 March 2014|newspaper=Slovak Spectator}}</ref>

In a promotional video during presidential election campaign in 2014, Fico said he grew up in a [[Roman Catholic]] family and that he considers himself a Catholic. He discussed his [[baptism]], [[holy communion]], [[confirmation (Catholic Church)|confirmation]] and how the Catholic faith had impacted his childhood.<ref name=rc>{{cite news|last=Terenzani|first=Michaela|title=Confusion arises over Fico's religious background|url=http://spectator.sme.sk/articles/view/52680/10/confusion_arises_over_ficos_religious_background.html|access-date=20 January 2014|newspaper=Slovakspectator.sk}}</ref>

===Andrej Kiska===
Andrej Kiska was the first candidate to officially announce his presidential candidacy and his billboards appeared around Slovakia long before the names of his competitors were known. He was officially running as an independent candidate and had no political background whatsoever. Prior to the first round Kiskas campaign mostly stressed his independence, including his financial independence, which mean that he didn't have to report to any political parties. Furthermore, he promoted his lack of political involvement as one of his strengths, while his website featured the slogan “The First Independent President”.<ref>{{cite news|last=Balogova|first=Beata|title=Who is Andrej Kiska?|url=http://spectator.sme.sk/articles/view/53130/2/who_is_andrej_kiska.html|access-date=31 March 2014|newspaper=Slovak Spectator}}</ref> Eventually, Andrej Kiska won the election on 29 March with nearly 60% of the popular vote, becoming the first president in the history of Slovak republic never to have been a member of the Communist Party, nor any other political party.<ref name="auto"/>

===Radoslav Procházka===
The young constitutional lawyer Prochazka, who campaigned as the conservative candidate, was formerly a member with the Christian Democratic Movement (KDH), but ran as an independent. His campaign mainly targeted the large catholic population, with issues such as abortion, same-sex marriage and traditional family values. His campaign also stressed his youth as a strength, as well as his background as a constitutional lawyer, with his official slogans reading: "A young president? Why not!" ({{langx|sk|Mladý prezident? Prečo nie!}}) and "Strong president. I will protect your rights!" ({{langx|sk|Silný prezident. Budem chrániť vaše práva!}})

===Peter Osuský===
The [[Freedom and Solidarity]] (SaS) party announced on 11 June 2013 that Peter Osuský would be their candidate for the presidency. Due to poor results in polls, Osuský decided to pull off his candidature. SaS reacted by supporting Radoslav Procházka for upcoming first round of elections.<ref>{{cite news|last=Balogova|first=Beata|title=Presidential candidate drops out|url=http://spectator.sme.sk/articles/view/52792/2/presidential_candidate_drops_out.html|access-date=31 March 2014|newspaper=Slovak Spectator}}</ref>

===Second round===
During the second round all independent candidates and some right-wing parties supported Kiska. These candidates secured more than 60% votes including the ones received by Kiska in first round. Fico secured 12% more votes than in the first round. High turn out in second round helped Kiska defeat Fico by 20 points.<ref>{{cite news|title=Sú známe oficiálne výsledky prezidentských volieb 2014|url=https://www.etrend.sk/ekonomika/su-zname-oficialne-vysledky-prezidentskych-volieb-2014.html|access-date=23 January 2018|language=sk|newspaper=eTrend}}</ref>

The second round of the election, which pitted Robert Fico against Andrej Kiska, saw the candidates engage in a series of televised presidential debates. Fico repeatedly accused Kiska of having links to the [[Church of Scientology]], but stopped short of calling him a member. Kiska however, confirmed his Catholic faith, and filed criminal charges against Fico.<ref>{{cite news|title=Fico accuses Kiska of Scientology ties; Kiska submits criminal complaint|url=http://spectator.sme.sk/articles/view/53349/10/fico_accuses_kiska_of_scientology_ties_kiska_submits_criminal_complaint.html|access-date=31 March 2014|newspaper=Slovak Spectator}}</ref> All the defeated candidates from the first round subsequently backed Kiska, while international social-democrats such as French president [[Francois Hollande]] and Speaker of the European Parliament [[Martin Schulz|Martin Schultz]] endorsed Fico.<ref>{{cite news|title= Hollande, Schulz support Fico in presidential run-off, Kiska prevails at home| url=http://spectator.sme.sk/articles/view/53422/10/hollande_schulz_support_fico_in_presidential_run_off_kiska_prevails_at_home.html|access-date=31 March 2014|newspaper=Slovak Spectator}}</ref>

